(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["dhyanabindu"]={"slug":"dhyanabindu","name":"Dhyānabindu","deva":"ध्यानबिन्दु","note":"selected verses","intro":["The Complete Text — All 105 Verses<br>Devanāgarī · IAST · English Translation<br>Word-by-Word for Key Verses · The Top 5 Commentaries<br><br>Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda · 39th in the Muktikā 108<br>\"The Point of Meditation\"","The Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad (ध्यानबिन्दूपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad of the Meditation-Point\") is one of the four Bindu Upaniṣads (Tejo-, Nāda-, Dhyāna-, Amṛta-Bindu). It is attached to the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda and is the 39th of the 108 Upaniṣads in the Muktikā's canonical list. The text consists of 105 verses in a single continuous flow (no chapter divisions) covering: dhyāna-yoga's power, the analysis of OM, nāda-yoga, the 14 nāḍīs, the 6 cakras, prāṇāyāma, the Khecarī mudrā, the three bandhas, and the culmination in self-recognition."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad (ध्यानबिन्दूपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad of the Meditation-Point\") is one of the four Bindu Upaniṣads (Tejo-, Nāda-, Dhyāna-, Amṛta-Bindu). It is attached to the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda and is the 39th of the 108 Upaniṣads in the Muktikā's canonical list. The text consists of 105 verses in a single continuous flow (no chapter divisions) covering: dhyāna-yoga's power, the analysis of OM, nāda-yoga, the 14 nāḍīs, the 6 cakras, prāṇāyāma, the Khecarī mudrā, the three bandhas, and the culmination in self-recognition.","Its name reflects its central practice: the bindu — the \"point\" — of dhyāna (meditation). The bindu is both the seed-point of sound (the half-mātrā at the end of OM's M-sound) and the seed-point at the brow-center where awareness collects. The text's opening verse declares the practice's efficacy:","\"Even if sin be vast as a mountain and extending over many leagues — it is destroyed by Dhyāna-yoga; no other destruction is ever effective.\" (v. 1)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. Place in the Bindu-Upaniṣad Family","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The four Bindu Upaniṣads form a thematic family within the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads:","<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Upaniṣad</td><td>Bindu type</td><td>Focus</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Tejo-Bindu</td><td>Light-point</td><td>Tejo-meditation + sustained Advaita (462 verses, longest)</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Nāda-Bindu</td><td>Sound-point</td><td>The inner sound, Praṇava analysis</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Dhyāna-Bindu</td><td>Meditation-point</td><td>Comprehensive yoga manual (this text, 105 verses)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Amṛta-Bindu</td><td>Nectar-point</td><td>Mind's twofold nature (shortest, ~22 verses)</td></tr></table>","Of these, the Dhyāna Bindu is the most practically comprehensive — it integrates praṇava-yoga, nāda-yoga, cakra-yoga, prāṇāyāma, and kuṇḍalinī-yoga in a single text. Where Tejo Bindu remains in the philosophical-Advaitic dimension, Dhyāna Bindu is unmistakably technical: it teaches HOW."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Structural Overview","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Dhyāna Bindu's thematic flow across its 105 verses"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The Power of Dhyāna-Yoga (vv. 1-8)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The text opens with a striking claim about dhyāna-yoga's power, and proceeds to establish the universality of Brahman in all things through five poetic analogies (vv. 5-7):","Fragrance in a flower (puṣpa-madhye gandha)","Ghee in milk (payo-madhye ghṛta)","Oil in sesame (tila-madhye taila)","Gold in stones (pāṣāṇeṣv iva kāñcana)","Beads on a thread (sūtra ivātmani)","Each analogy emphasizes the same insight: Brahman is everywhere immanent — pervading what appears separate, yet not visible to ordinary sight. By dhyāna, what is hidden becomes manifest."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The OM (Praṇava) Analysis (vv. 9-22)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The three syllables of OM with cosmic correspondences","A substantial section of the Dhyāna Bindu (vv. 9-22) is devoted to the analysis of OM. The text systematically associates each of the three syllables (A, U, M) with:","A realm (earth / atmosphere / heaven)","A natural element (fire / wind / sun)","A Veda (Ṛk / Yajur / Sāma)","A vyāhṛti (bhūḥ / bhuvaḥ / svaḥ)","A deity (Brahmā / Viṣṇu / Maheśvara)","A guṇa-color (yellow rajas / white sattva / black tamas)","The text states (v. 13): \"Eight-limbed, four-footed, three-placed, five-deities\" — describing the praṇava's structural fullness. Verse 17 gives the practical formula: short OM burns sin, long OM grants prosperity, half-mātrā (the silence after M) grants liberation."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The Bow-Arrow-Target Image (vv. 14-15)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The classical bow-arrow-target image of meditation","One of the most celebrated images of meditation in all Upaniṣadic literature:","\"praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hyātmā brahma tal-lakṣyam ucyate / apramattena veddhavyaṃ śaravat tanmayo bhavet\"<br>— \"The praṇava is the bow, the Self is the arrow, Brahman is said to be the target. It is to be pierced by the unwavering one; one becomes filled with That, as an arrow becomes embedded.\"","This same image appears in the Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad 2.2.4. Here the Dhyāna Bindu reaffirms it as a central meditation: the OM-mantra is the means, the Self is what travels, Brahman is the target, and the practitioner — like a skilled archer — must be apramatta (undistracted, unwavering)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The Six Cakras (vv. 43-46)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The six-cakra enumeration of the Dhyāna Bindu","The Dhyāna Bindu contains one of the EARLIEST clear enumerations of the six cakras in the Upaniṣadic literature. The cakra system that would become foundational in later Tantra is here in its classical form:","1. Mūlādhāra (perineum) — the foundation","2. Svādhiṣṭhāna (lower abdomen) — the next","3. Maṇipūra(ka) (below the navel)","4. Anāhata (in the heart)","5. Viśuddhi (above the throat)","6. Ājñā (between the brows)","+ Brahmarandhra / Sahasrāra (above the crown)","Although the Dhyāna Bindu does not name the cakras \"lotuses\" with their petal-counts (a Tantric elaboration), the basic system of six wheels + Brahmarandhra is here established. The yogi pierces each cakra through dhyāna and prāṇāyāma until kuṇḍalinī rises through Suṣumnā to the Brahmarandhra (vv. 60-62, 95-96)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. The 14 Nāḍīs (vv. 38-42)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The 14 nāḍīs — same enumeration as Śāṇḍilya Upaniṣad","The Dhyāna Bindu names the same 14 principal nāḍīs as the Śāṇḍilya Upaniṣad — Iḍā, Piṅgalā, Suṣumnā plus 11 others. The text states (v. 41): \"Of nāḍīs, ten are principal; among ten thousand are 72,000 minor nāḍīs in the body.\" The \"72,000\" figure becomes standard in all later yoga literature.","Verse 42 introduces the kuṇḍalinī: \"the kuṇḍalinī coiled three and a half times rests at Mūlādhāra\" — one of the earliest Upaniṣadic statements of this image, which is otherwise extensively developed in Tantric texts."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"9. The Khecarī Mudrā (vv. 68-79)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Dhyāna Bindu contains one of the most detailed Upaniṣadic descriptions of Khecarī-mudrā — the practice of turning the tongue back into the cranial cavity above the palate, with the gaze fixed between the brows. The text claims:","By Khecarī: sleep, hunger, thirst, and aging are conquered (v. 70)","The yogi attaining Khecarī-state is freed from disease, karma, saṃsāra (v. 71)","It enables drinking the immortal nectar that flows from the union of Śiva and Śakti at the brahmarandhra (v. 76)","Through it, all siddhis arise as servants (v. 78)","This same mudrā is later given vast treatment in the Khecarī-Vidyā and Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā. Dhyāna Bindu's account is foundational."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Key Verses with Word-by-Word","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Ten foundational verses with complete grammatical analysis."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Verse 1","title":"The Opening Declaration","sa":["यदि शैलसमं पापं विस्तीर्णं बहुयोजनम् ।","भिद्यते ध्यानयोगेन नान्यो भेदः कदाचन ॥"],"iast":["yadi śailasamaṃ pāpaṃ vistīrṇaṃ bahuyojanam |","bhidyate dhyānayogena nānyo bhedaḥ kadācana ||"],"words":[["yadi","particle","even if / suppose"],["śaila-samam","cmpd. n. nom. sg.","mountain-like / equal to a mountain"],["pāpam","n. nom. sg.","sin"],["vistīrṇam","p.p. n. nom. sg.","spread out / extensive"],["bahu-yojanam","cmpd. n. nom. sg.","[over] many leagues (yojanas)"],["bhidyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is split / broken"],["dhyāna-yogena","cmpd. m. instr. sg.","by Dhyāna-yoga (meditation-discipline)"],["na anyaḥ","neg. + m. nom. sg.","no other"],["bhedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","destroyer / piercer"],["kadācana","adv.","ever / at any time"]],"lit":"Even if sin be vast as a mountain and extending over many leagues — it is split apart by Dhyāna-yoga; no other destroyer is ever [effective].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The text's opening claim. Note the striking strong-form verb \"bhidyate\" — \"is pierced/split/broken\" — chosen specifically because the verse compares sin to a mountain. Only dhyāna-yoga can break this mountain. The grammatical structure makes the claim absolute: \"no other (na anyaḥ) destroyer ever (kadācana).\""]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Verse 5-6","title":"The Five Analogies (excerpted)","sa":["पुष्पमध्ये यथा गन्धः पयोमध्ये यथा घृतम् ।","तिलमध्ये यथा तैलं पाषाणाष्विव काञ्चनम् ॥"],"iast":["puṣpa-madhye yathā gandhaḥ payo-madhye yathā ghṛtam |","tila-madhye yathā tailaṃ pāṣāṇāṣv iva kāñcanam ||"],"words":[["puṣpa-madhye","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the middle of a flower"],["yathā","particle","just as"],["gandhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","fragrance"],["payo-madhye","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the middle of milk"],["ghṛtam","n. nom. sg.","ghee"],["tila-madhye","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the middle of sesame"],["tailam","n. nom. sg.","oil"],["pāṣāṇeṣu","m. loc. pl.","in stones"],["iva","particle","like"],["kāñcanam","n. nom. sg.","gold"]],"lit":"As fragrance in a flower, as ghee in milk, as oil in sesame, as gold in stones — [so the Self is in all beings].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Four similes establishing the immanence-doctrine. Each analogy shows something present-yet-hidden, manifestable through the right method (pressing, churning, mining). Brahman is similarly hidden in beings — manifestable through dhyāna. Verse 6 completes the comparison: \"Thus all beings are like beads on a thread in the Self.\""]},{"ref":"Verse 9","title":"OM is to be Meditated","sa":["ओमित्येकाक्षरं ब्रह्म ध्येयं सर्वमुमुक्षिभिः ।","पृथिव्यग्निश्च ऋग्वेदो भूरित्येव पितामहः ॥"],"iast":["om ity ekākṣaraṃ brahma dhyeyaṃ sarva-mumukṣibhiḥ |","pṛthivy-agniś ca ṛgvedo bhūr ity eva pitāmahaḥ ||"],"words":[["om iti","—","\"OM\" — thus"],["eka-akṣaram","cmpd. n. nom. sg.","one-syllabled / imperishable-one"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["dhyeyam","gerundive n.","to be meditated upon"],["sarva-mumukṣibhiḥ","cmpd. m. instr. pl.","by all seekers of liberation"],["pṛthivī agniḥ ca","—","Earth and Fire"],["ṛgvedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Ṛgveda"],["bhūḥ iti eva","—","\"BHŪḤ\" — thus alone"],["pitāmahaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Grandfather (Brahmā)"]],"lit":"OM — the one-syllabled / imperishable Brahman — is to be meditated upon by all seekers of liberation. Earth, Fire, the Ṛgveda, the syllable \"BHŪḤ\" — [these are presided over by] the Grandfather (Brahmā).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Verse 9 begins the systematic analysis of the praṇava's correspondences. The pun in \"ekākṣaram\" is intentional: \"the one-syllabled\" AND \"the one-imperishable\" — akṣara meaning both \"syllable\" and \"imperishable.\" This is a foundational Vedānta-pun: OM is both the syllable and the indestructible reality. Verses 10-12 continue with U and M correspondences."]},{"ref":"Verse 14","title":"The Bow-Arrow-Target","sa":["ओङ्कारं यो न जानाति ब्रह्मणो न भवेत्तु सः ।","प्रणवो धनुः शरो ह्यात्मा ब्रह्म तल्लक्ष्यमुच्यते ॥"],"iast":["oṅkāraṃ yo na jānāti brahmaṇo na bhavet tu saḥ |","praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hy ātmā brahma tal-lakṣyam ucyate ||"],"words":[["oṅkāram","m. acc. sg.","the OM-syllable"],["yaḥ na jānāti","—","who does not know"],["brahmaṇaḥ","n. gen. sg.","[a knower] of Brahman"],["na bhavet tu saḥ","—","he does not become"],["praṇavaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the praṇava (OM)"],["dhanuḥ","n. nom. sg.","the bow"],["śaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the arrow"],["hi ātmā","—","is verily the Self"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","Brahman"],["tat lakṣyam","—","that target"],["ucyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is said to be"]],"lit":"He who does not know OM does not become a knower of Brahman. The praṇava is the bow, the Self is the arrow, Brahman is said to be the target.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The bow-arrow-target image, which also occurs in Muṇḍaka Up. 2.2.4. The verse first warns: without knowing OM, Brahman-knowledge is impossible. Then gives the famous image: praṇava is the means (bow), Self is what travels (arrow), Brahman is the destination (target). Verse 15 continues: \"It is to be pierced by the unwavering one; one becomes filled with That as an arrow becomes embedded.\""]},{"ref":"Verse 19","title":"The Heart-Lotus Meditation","sa":["हृत्पद्मकर्णिकामध्ये स्थिरदीपनिभाकृतिम् ।","अङ्गुष्ठमात्रमचलं ध्यायेदोङ्कारमीश्वरम् ॥"],"iast":["hṛt-padma-karṇikā-madhye sthira-dīpa-nibhākṛtim |","aṅguṣṭha-mātram acalaṃ dhyāyed oṅkāram īśvaram ||"],"words":[["hṛt-padma-karṇikā-madhye","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the calyx-middle of the heart-lotus"],["sthira-dīpa-nibha-ākṛtim","cmpd. f. acc. sg.","of the form of a steady lamp"],["aṅguṣṭha-mātram","cmpd. n. acc. sg.","thumb-sized"],["acalam","n. acc. sg.","motionless"],["dhyāyet","3rd sg. opt.","should meditate"],["oṅkāram","m. acc. sg.","the OM-syllable"],["īśvaram","m. acc. sg.","as the Lord"]],"lit":"In the calyx of the heart-lotus, with the form of a steady lamp, of the size of a thumb, motionless — one should meditate on OM as the Lord.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A precise meditation instruction. The visualization: the heart is a lotus; in its center (karṇikā = pericarp/calyx), a steady, unflickering lamp-flame, thumb-sized. This is OM-as-Īśvara. The image of \"thumb-sized\" (aṅguṣṭha-mātra) connects to many other Upaniṣadic descriptions of the Self in the heart (Kaṭha 2.1.12, Śvetāśvatara 3.13)."]},{"ref":"Verse 21","title":"The Three Deities of Prāṇāyāma","sa":["ब्रह्मा पूरक इत्युक्तो विष्णुः कुम्भक उच्यते ।","रेचो रुद्र इति प्रोक्तः प्राणायामस्य देवताः ॥"],"iast":["brahmā pūraka ity ukto viṣṇuḥ kumbhaka ucyate |","recho rudra iti proktaḥ prāṇāyāmasya devatāḥ ||"],"words":[["brahmā","m. nom. sg.","Brahmā (the creator)"],["pūrakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","pūraka (inhalation)"],["iti uktaḥ","—","is said to be thus"],["viṣṇuḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viṣṇu (the sustainer)"],["kumbhakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","kumbhaka (retention)"],["ucyate","3rd sg. pres. pass.","is called"],["rechaḥ","m. nom. sg.","recaka (exhalation)"],["rudraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Rudra (the dissolver)"],["iti proktaḥ","—","is so declared"],["prāṇāyāmasya devatāḥ","—","the deities of prāṇāyāma"]],"lit":"Brahmā is declared as pūraka (inhalation); Viṣṇu is called kumbhaka (retention); Rudra is declared as recaka (exhalation) — these are the deities of prāṇāyāma.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A beautiful integration of breath-discipline with the Hindu Trinity. The cosmic functions map onto the breath: inhalation = creation (Brahmā), retention = sustenance (Viṣṇu), exhalation = dissolution (Rudra/Śiva). This makes every breath a recapitulation of cosmic activity. The same identification appears in many Tantric texts."]},{"ref":"Verse 22","title":"The Self as Lower Wood","sa":["आत्मानमरणिं कृत्वा प्रणवं चोत्तरारणिम् ।","ध्याननिर्मथनाभ्यासाद्दहत्पाशं विचक्षणः ॥"],"iast":["ātmānam araṇiṃ kṛtvā praṇavaṃ cottara-araṇim |","dhyāna-nirmathana-abhyāsād dahet pāśaṃ vicakṣaṇaḥ ||"],"words":[["ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","the Self"],["araṇim","f. acc. sg.","lower-wood (of fire-churning)"],["kṛtvā","gerund","having made"],["praṇavam","m. acc. sg.","the praṇava (OM)"],["uttara-araṇim","cmpd. f. acc. sg.","upper-wood"],["dhyāna-nirmathana-abhyāsāt","cmpd. abl.","by the practice of churning [through] meditation"],["dahet","3rd sg. opt.","should burn"],["pāśam","m. acc. sg.","the bond / fetter"],["vicakṣaṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the wise one"]],"lit":"Making the Self the lower-wood [of fire-churning] and the praṇava the upper-wood — by the practice of churning through meditation, the wise one should burn the bondage.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A magnificent image drawn from the Vedic fire-ritual. In the original ritual, two wooden sticks (araṇis) were rubbed together to produce sacred fire. Here the Self is the lower stick, OM is the upper stick, dhyāna is the rubbing — and the resultant fire burns the bondage of saṃsāra. This image appears nearly identically in Śvetāśvatara 1.14."]},{"ref":"Verse 42","title":"Kuṇḍalinī Coiled Three and a Half Times","sa":["सोऽग्निसूर्ययोर्मध्ये सोमस्थाने प्रतिष्ठितः ।","सार्धत्रिवलयाकारा कुण्डलिन्यथ देवता ॥"],"iast":["so 'gni-sūryayor madhye soma-sthāne pratiṣṭhitaḥ |","sārdha-tri-valaya-ākārā kuṇḍaliny atha devatā ||"],"words":[["saḥ","m. nom. sg.","he/that"],["agni-sūryayoḥ madhye","—","in the middle of fire and sun"],["soma-sthāne","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the soma-place"],["pratiṣṭhitaḥ","p.p. m. nom.","established"],["sa-ardha-tri-valaya-ākārā","cmpd. f. nom. sg.","of the form of three-and-a-half coils"],["kuṇḍalinī","f. nom. sg.","Kuṇḍalinī"],["atha devatā","—","is the deity"]],"lit":"[The yogi is] established between fire and sun, in the soma-place. Kuṇḍalinī is the deity, of the form of three-and-a-half coils.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["One of the earliest Upaniṣadic statements of kuṇḍalinī as \"coiled three and a half times\" (sa-ardha-tri-valaya). This image becomes universal in later Tantra. The \"three and a half\" coils refer either to the three guṇas + the half-mātrā (OM's silent end), or to the three nāḍīs + Suṣumnā. The yogi establishes himself \"between fire (Piṅgalā/sun) and sun (which becomes moon in Iḍā)\" in the central soma-channel (Suṣumnā)."]},{"ref":"Verse 69-70","title":"Khecarī Mudrā","sa":["कपालकुहरे जिह्वा प्रविष्टा विपरीतगा ।","भ्रुवोरन्तर्गता दृष्टिर्मुद्रा भवति खेचरी ॥"],"iast":["kapāla-kuhare jihvā praviṣṭā viparīta-gā |","bhruvor antar-gatā dṛṣṭir mudrā bhavati khecarī ||"],"words":[["kapāla-kuhare","cmpd. n. loc. sg.","in the cranial cavity"],["jihvā","f. nom. sg.","the tongue"],["praviṣṭā","p.p. f. nom. sg.","entered"],["viparīta-gā","cmpd. f. nom. sg.","going in reverse"],["bhruvoḥ antar-gatā","—","gone between the eyebrows"],["dṛṣṭiḥ","f. nom. sg.","the gaze"],["mudrā","f. nom. sg.","mudrā / seal"],["bhavati","3rd sg. pres.","becomes"],["khecarī","f. nom. sg.","Khecarī (\"sky-walker\")"]],"lit":"The tongue, reversed-going, entered into the cranial cavity; the gaze fixed between the brows — this mudrā becomes Khecarī.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The classical Khecarī-mudrā instruction. The tongue is \"reversed-going\" (viparīta-gā): turned backward and upward into the cavity above the palate. Simultaneously, the eyes are fixed at the brow-center (ājñā cakra). The combination produces khecarī — literally \"sky-walking\" — the supreme yogic seal. The same description appears in the Vijñāna Bhairava Tantra and the Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā."]},{"ref":"Verse 105","title":"The Closing Realization","sa":["स्वात्मानं पुरुषं पश्येन्मनस्तत्र लयं गतम् ।","य एवं वेद पुरुषः स कैवल्यं समश्नुत इत्युपनिषत् ॥"],"iast":["sv-ātmānaṃ puruṣaṃ paśyen manas tatra layaṃ gatam |","ya evaṃ veda puruṣaḥ sa kaivalyaṃ samaśnuta iti upaniṣat ||"],"words":[["sva-ātmānam","cmpd. m. acc. sg.","his own Self"],["puruṣam","m. acc. sg.","the Person"],["paśyet","3rd sg. opt.","should see"],["manaḥ","n. nom. sg.","the mind"],["tatra","adv.","there"],["layam gatam","—","gone to dissolution"],["yaḥ evam veda","—","whoever knows thus"],["puruṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the person"],["saḥ kaivalyam samaśnute","—","he attains kaivalya"],["iti upaniṣat","—","thus says the Upaniṣad"]],"lit":"One sees his own Self, the Person — there the mind has gone to dissolution. Whoever knows thus, the Person, attains kaivalya — thus says the Upaniṣad.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The closing verse. The result of the entire 104-verse practice is summarized: the practitioner sees his own Self as the Person (Puruṣa) — the mind, the medium of seeing, dissolves at that recognition. \"Yaḥ evam veda\" — \"whoever knows thus\" — the Upaniṣadic formula for the one who has received the teaching. Such a one attains kaivalya — the supreme aloneness, the fullness of Self."]},{"ref":"11. Opening Invocation","title":"","sa":["ध्यात्वा यद्ब्रह्ममात्रं ते स्वावशेषधिया ययुः ।","योगतत्त्वज्ञानफलं तत्स्वमात्रं विचिन्तये ॥","ॐ सह नाववतु ॥ सह नौ भुनक्तु ॥","सह वीर्यं करवावहै ॥","तेजस्विनावधीतमस्तु मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["dhyātvā yadbrahmamātraṃ te svāvaśeṣadhiyā yayuḥ |","yogatattvajñānaphalaṃ tatsvamātraṃ vicintaye ||","oṃ saha nāvavatu || saha nau bhunaktu ||","saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai ||","tejasvināvadhītamastu mā vidviṣāvahai ||","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"Maṅgala-śloka: \"Having meditated on Brahman-only, by intellect with [only the] Self-remaining, [the sages] went [there]; the fruit of yoga-tattva-knowledge — that very [Self] I contemplate.\"  Śānti Mantra: \"OM. May He protect us both; may He nourish us both; may we both work with great vigor; may our learning be brilliant; may we not hate. OM. Peace. Peace. Peace.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda)","u":[{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 1","title":"","sa":["यदि शैलसमं पापं विस्तीर्णं बहुयोजनम् । ।","भिद्यते ध्यानयोगेन नान्यो भेदः कदाचन ।"],"iast":["yadi śailasamaṃ pāpaṃ vistīrṇaṃ bahuyojanam | |","bhidyate dhyānayogena nānyo bhedaḥ kadācana |"],"words":[],"lit":"Even if sin be vast as a mountain and extending over many leagues — it is destroyed by Dhyāna-yoga; no other destruction is ever (effective).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 2","title":"","sa":["बीजाक्षरं परं बिन्दुं नादो तस्योपरि स्थितम् । ।","सशब्दं चाक्षरे क्षीणे निःशब्दं परमं पदम् ।"],"iast":["bījākṣaraṃ paraṃ binduṃ nādo tasyopari sthitam | |","saśabdaṃ cākṣare kṣīṇe niḥśabdaṃ paramaṃ padam |"],"words":[],"lit":"The seed-syllable is supreme; the bindu is the supreme; nāda stands upon them. When sound-with-form ceases in the imperishable, the soundless supreme state is reached.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 3","title":"","sa":["अनाहतं तु यच्छब्दं तस्य शब्दस्य यत्परम् । ।","तत्परं विन्दते यस्तु स योगी छिन्नसंशयः ।"],"iast":["anāhataṃ tu yacchabdaṃ tasya śabdasya yatparam | |","tatparaṃ vindate yastu sa yogī chinnasaṃśayaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Whatever sound is unstruck (anāhata) — and that which is beyond [even] that sound — whoever finds that supreme, that yogi is freed from doubt.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 4","title":"","sa":["वालाग्रशतसाहस्रं तस्य भागस्य भागिनः । ।","तस्य भागस्य भागार्धं तत्क्षये तु निरञ्जनम् ।"],"iast":["vālāgraśatasāhasraṃ tasya bhāgasya bhāginaḥ | |","tasya bhāgasya bhāgārdhaṃ tatkṣaye tu nirañjanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Like a hundred-thousand-fold split of a hair-tip; the half of even that fraction's fraction — at its dissolution alone is the Stainless.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 5","title":"","sa":["पुष्पमध्ये यथा गन्धः पयोमध्ये यथा घृतम् । ।","तिलमध्ये यथा तैलं पाषाणाष्विव काञ्चनम् ।"],"iast":["puṣpamadhye yathā gandhaḥ payomadhye yathā ghṛtam | |","tilamadhye yathā tailaṃ pāṣāṇāṣviva kāñcanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"As fragrance in a flower, as ghee in milk, as oil in sesame, as gold in stones —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 6","title":"","sa":["एवं सर्वाणि भूतानि मणौ सूत्र इवात्मनि । ।","स्थिरबुद्धिरसंमूढो ब्रह्मविद्ब्रह्मणिस्थितः ।"],"iast":["evaṃ sarvāṇi bhūtāni maṇau sūtra ivātmani | |","sthirabuddhirasaṃmūḍho brahmavidbrahmaṇisthitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"So all beings — like beads strung on a thread in the Self — established with steady intellect, undeluded, the knower of Brahman abides in Brahman.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 7","title":"","sa":["तिलानां तु यथा तैलं पुष्पे गन्ध इवाश्रितः । ।","पुरुषस्य शरीरे तु सबाह्याभ्यन्तरे स्थितः ।"],"iast":["tilānāṃ tu yathā tailaṃ puṣpe gandha ivāśritaḥ | |","puruṣasya śarīre tu sabāhyābhyantare sthitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"As oil pervades sesame seeds, as fragrance pervades a flower — so [Brahman] is established in the human body, both externally and internally.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 8","title":"","sa":["वृक्षं तु सकलं विद्याच्छाया तस्यैव निष्कला । ।","सकले निष्कले भावे सर्वत्रात्मा व्यवस्थितः ।"],"iast":["vṛkṣaṃ tu sakalaṃ vidyācchāyā tasyaiva niṣkalā | |","sakale niṣkale bhāve sarvatrātmā vyavasthitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Know the entire tree as 'sakala' (with-parts); its shadow as 'niṣkala' (partless). In both with-parts and partless states, the Self is everywhere established.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 9","title":"","sa":["ओमित्येकाक्षरं ब्रह्म ध्येयं सर्वमुमुक्षिभिः । ।","पृथिव्यग्निश्च ऋग्वेदो भूरित्येव पितामहः ।"],"iast":["omityekākṣaraṃ brahma dhyeyaṃ sarvamumukṣibhiḥ | |","pṛthivyagniśca ṛgvedo bhūrityeva pitāmahaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"OM — the one-syllabled Brahman — is to be meditated upon by all seekers of liberation. Earth, fire, the Ṛgveda, the syllable 'BHŪ' — these are [presided over by] the Grandfather (Brahmā).","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 10","title":"","sa":["अकारे तु लयं प्राप्ते प्रथमे प्रणवांशके । ।","अन्तरिक्षं यजुर्वायुर्भुवो विष्णुर्जनार्दनः ।"],"iast":["akāre tu layaṃ prāpte prathame praṇavāṃśake | |","antarikṣaṃ yajurvāyurbhuvo viṣṇurjanārdanaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the first part of the praṇava — the syllable A — is dissolved: atmosphere, Yajurveda, wind, [the syllable] 'BHUVAḤ', Viṣṇu (the awakener) [are involved].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 11","title":"","sa":["उकारे तु लयं प्राप्ते द्वितीये प्रणवांशके । ।","द्यौः सूर्यः सामवेदश्च स्वरित्येव महेश्वरः ।"],"iast":["ukāre tu layaṃ prāpte dvitīye praṇavāṃśake | |","dyauḥ sūryaḥ sāmavedaśca svarityeva maheśvaraḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the second part of the praṇava — the syllable U — is dissolved: heaven, sun, Sāmaveda, [the syllable] 'SVAḤ', Maheśvara [are involved].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 12","title":"","sa":["मकारे तु लयं प्राप्ते तृतीये प्रणवांशके । ।","अकारः पीतवर्णः  स्याद्रजोगुण उदीरितः ।"],"iast":["makāre tu layaṃ prāpte tṛtīye praṇavāṃśake | |","akāraḥ pītavarṇaḥ  syādrajoguṇa udīritaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the third part of the praṇava — the syllable M — is dissolved: the A-syllable would be of yellow color, declared as rajoguṇa.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 13","title":"","sa":["उकारः सात्त्विकः शुक्लो मकारः कृष्णतामसः । ।","अष्टाङ्गं च चतुष्पादं त्रिस्थानं पञ्चदैवतम् ।"],"iast":["ukāraḥ sāttvikaḥ śuklo makāraḥ kṛṣṇatāmasaḥ | |","aṣṭāṅgaṃ ca catuṣpādaṃ tristhānaṃ pañcadaivatam |"],"words":[],"lit":"U-syllable is sāttvic, white; M-syllable is black, tāmasic. Eight-limbed, four-footed, three-placed, five-deities is [the praṇava].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 14","title":"","sa":["ओङ्कारं यो न जानाति ब्रह्मणो न भवेत्तु सः । ।","प्रणवो धनुः शरो ह्यात्मा ब्रह्म तल्लक्ष्यमुच्यते ।"],"iast":["oṅkāraṃ yo na jānāti brahmaṇo na bhavettu saḥ | |","praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hyātmā brahma tallakṣyamucyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"He who does not know OM is not [a knower] of Brahman. The praṇava is the bow, the arrow is the Self, Brahman is said to be the target.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 15","title":"","sa":["अप्रमत्तेन वेद्धव्यं शरवत्तन्मयो भवेत् । ।","निवर्तन्ते क्रियाः सर्वास्तस्मिन्दृष्टे परावरे ।"],"iast":["apramattena veddhavyaṃ śaravattanmayo bhavet | |","nivartante kriyāḥ sarvāstasmindṛṣṭe parāvare |"],"words":[],"lit":"It is to be pierced by the unwavering one; one becomes filled with That as an arrow becomes [embedded]. When That higher-than-the-higher is seen — all actions cease.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 16","title":"","sa":["ओङ्कारप्रभवा देवा ओङ्कारप्रभवाः स्वराः । ।","ओङ्कारप्रभवं सर्वं त्रैलोक्यं सचराचरम् ।"],"iast":["oṅkāraprabhavā devā oṅkāraprabhavāḥ svarāḥ | |","oṅkāraprabhavaṃ sarvaṃ trailokyaṃ sacarācaram |"],"words":[],"lit":"From OM the gods arose; from OM the meters; from OM the entire three-worlds, with moving and unmoving.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 17","title":"","sa":["ह्रस्वो दहति पापानि दीर्घः सम्पत्प्रदोऽव्ययः । ।","अर्धमात्रा समायुक्तः प्रणवो मोक्षदायकः ।"],"iast":["hrasvo dahati pāpāni dīrghaḥ sampatprado'vyayaḥ | |","ardhamātrā samāyuktaḥ praṇavo mokṣadāyakaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Short [OM] burns away sins; long [OM] is the eternal giver of prosperity; with half-mātrā joined, the praṇava is the giver of liberation.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 18","title":"","sa":["तैलधारामिवाच्छिन्नं दीर्घघण्टानिनादवत् । ।","अवाच्यं प्रणवस्याग्रं यस्तं वेद स वेदवित् ।"],"iast":["tailadhārāmivācchinnaṃ dīrghaghaṇṭāninādavat | |","avācyaṃ praṇavasyāgraṃ yastaṃ veda sa vedavit |"],"words":[],"lit":"Unbroken like a stream of oil, like the prolonged tolling of a bell — the indescribable [last] part of the praṇava — he who knows that, knows the Veda.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 19","title":"","sa":["हृत्पद्मकर्णिकामध्ये स्थिरदीपनिभाकृतिम् । ।","अङ्गुष्ठमात्रमचलं ध्यायेदोङ्कारमीश्वरम् ।"],"iast":["hṛtpadmakarṇikāmadhye sthiradīpanibhākṛtim | |","aṅguṣṭhamātramacalaṃ dhyāyedoṅkāramīśvaram |"],"words":[],"lit":"In the calyx of the heart-lotus, with the form of a steady lamp, of the size of a thumb, motionless — one should meditate on OM as the Lord.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 20","title":"","sa":["इडया वायुमापुर्य पूरयित्वोदरस्थितम् । ।","ओङ्कारं देहमध्यस्थं ध्यायेज्ज्वालवलीवृतम् ।"],"iast":["iḍayā vāyumāpurya pūrayitvodarasthitam | |","oṅkāraṃ dehamadhyasthaṃ dhyāyejjvālavalīvṛtam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Filling with air through Iḍā, having filled [the air] that abides in the abdomen — one should meditate on OM in the middle of the body, surrounded by a network of flames.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 21","title":"","sa":["ब्रह्मा पूरक इत्युक्तो विष्णुः कुम्भक उच्यते । ।","रेचो रुद्र इति प्रोक्तः प्राणायामस्य देवताः ।"],"iast":["brahmā pūraka ityukto viṣṇuḥ kumbhaka ucyate | |","reco rudra iti proktaḥ prāṇāyāmasya devatāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Brahmā is declared as pūraka (inhalation); Viṣṇu is called kumbhaka (retention); Rudra is declared as recaka (exhalation) — these are the deities of prāṇāyāma.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 22","title":"","sa":["आत्मानमरणिं कृत्वा प्रणवं चोत्तरारणिम् । ।","ध्याननिर्मथनाभ्यासादेव पश्येन्निगूढवत् ।"],"iast":["ātmānamaraṇiṃ kṛtvā praṇavaṃ cottarāraṇim | |","dhyānanirmathanābhyāsādeva paśyennigūḍhavat |"],"words":[],"lit":"Making the Self the lower wood (araṇi), the praṇava the upper wood — by churning of dhyāna-practice, one should burn the [karmic] sheaths.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 23","title":"","sa":["ओङ्कारध्वनिनादेन वायोः संहरणान्तिकम् । ।","यावद्बलं समादध्यात्सम्यङ्नादलयावधि ।"],"iast":["oṅkāradhvaninādena vāyoḥ saṃharaṇāntikam | |","yāvadbalaṃ samādadhyātsamyaṅnādalayāvadhi |"],"words":[],"lit":"Until tranquilization through the wakeful state, until samādhi-bondage — until the limit of nāda-dissolution — one should fully maintain [practice] to one's strength.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 24","title":"","sa":["गमागमस्थं गमनादिशून्य\\- ।","मोङ्कारमेकं रविकोटिदीप्तिम् । ।","पश्यन्ति ये सर्वजनान्तरस्थं ।","हंसात्मकं ते विरजा भवन्ति ।"],"iast":["gamāgamasthaṃ gamanādiśūnya\\- |","moṅkāramekaṃ ravikoṭidīptim | |","paśyanti ye sarvajanāntarasthaṃ |","haṃsātmakaṃ te virajā bhavanti |"],"words":[],"lit":"Like a bee drinking honey, paying no heed to fragrance — so the mind, ever engaged in nāda, longs not for sense-objects.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 25","title":"","sa":["यन्मनस्त्रिजगत्सृष्टिस्थितिव्यसनकर्मकृत् । ।","तन्मनो विलयं याति तद्विष्णोः परमं पदम् ।"],"iast":["yanmanastrijagatsṛṣṭisthitivyasanakarmakṛt | |","tanmano vilayaṃ yāti tadviṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Bound by the goad of nāda — the elephant of mind, wandering in the garden of sense-objects — has its restless tendency suppressed.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 26","title":"","sa":["अष्टपत्रं तु हृत्पद्मं द्वात्रिंशत्केसरान्वितम् । ।","तस्य मध्ये स्थितो भानुर्भानुमध्यगतः शशी ।"],"iast":["aṣṭapatraṃ tu hṛtpadmaṃ dvātriṃśatkesarānvitam | |","tasya madhye sthito bhānurbhānumadhyagataḥ śaśī |"],"words":[],"lit":"Caught by the snare of nāda — having abandoned its fickleness — the deer of mind becomes motionless, like the cobra absorbed in song.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 27","title":"","sa":["शशिमध्यगतो वह्निर्वह्निमध्यगता प्रभा । ।","प्रभामध्यगतं पीठं नानारत्नप्रवेष्टितम् ।"],"iast":["śaśimadhyagato vahnirvahnimadhyagatā prabhā | |","prabhāmadhyagataṃ pīṭhaṃ nānāratnapraveṣṭitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Iron, by the alchemical process of mercury, becomes gold — so the mind, by sandhāna (joining with the nāda-bindu), becomes Brahman.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 28","title":"","sa":["तस्य मध्यगतं देवं वासुदेवं निरञ्जनम् । ।","श्रीवत्सकौस्तुभोरस्कं मुक्तामणिविभूषितम् ।"],"iast":["tasya madhyagataṃ devaṃ vāsudevaṃ nirañjanam | |","śrīvatsakaustubhoraskaṃ muktāmaṇivibhūṣitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"As a snake, biting its sleeping yogi — into the dissolution-Self enters; the yogi who has become Self-form — that wise one — moves freely.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 29","title":"","sa":["शुद्धस्फटिकसंकाशं चन्द्रकोटिसमप्रभम् । ।","एवं ध्यायेन्महाविष्णुमेवं वा विनयान्वितः ।"],"iast":["śuddhasphaṭikasaṃkāśaṃ candrakoṭisamaprabham | |","evaṃ dhyāyenmahāviṣṇumevaṃ vā vinayānvitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"In the middle of the heart-lotus is the soul (jīva), of the size of a tail-bristle; meditating on OM, ascending to its end, becomes one with that Lord.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 30","title":"","sa":["अतसीपुष्पसंकाशं नाभिस्थाने प्रतिष्ठितम् । ।","चतुर्भुजं महाविष्णुं पूरकेण विचिन्तयेत् ।"],"iast":["atasīpuṣpasaṃkāśaṃ nābhisthāne pratiṣṭhitam | |","caturbhujaṃ mahāviṣṇuṃ pūrakeṇa vicintayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Brahma-knot stands at the heart-region; Viṣṇu-knot at the throat-region; Rudra-knot at the brow-region — these are pierced by the dhyāna-flame.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 31","title":"","sa":["कुम्भकेन हृदिस्थाने चिन्तयेत्कमलासनम् । ।","ब्रह्माणं रक्तगौराभं चतुर्वक्त्रं पितामहम् ।"],"iast":["kumbhakena hṛdisthāne cintayetkamalāsanam | |","brahmāṇaṃ raktagaurābhaṃ caturvaktraṃ pitāmaham |"],"words":[],"lit":"The body of nine doors, with three pillars, five witnesses, one dweller — he who knows this, that one is a yogi.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 32","title":"","sa":["रेचकेन तु विद्यात्मा ललाटस्थं त्रिलोचनम् । ।","शुद्धस्फटिकसंकाशं निष्कलं पापनाशनम् ।"],"iast":["recakena tu vidyātmā lalāṭasthaṃ trilocanam | |","śuddhasphaṭikasaṃkāśaṃ niṣkalaṃ pāpanāśanam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Six wheels, sixteen supports, three targets, five voids — this is what one should know inwardly in the body, O Knower-of-Brahman.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 33","title":"","sa":["अञ्जपत्रमधःपुष्पमूर्ध्वनालमधोमुखम् । ।","कदलीपुष्पसंकाशं सर्ववेदमयं शिवम् ।"],"iast":["añjapatramadhaḥpuṣpamūrdhvanālamadhomukham | |","kadalīpuṣpasaṃkāśaṃ sarvavedamayaṃ śivam |"],"words":[],"lit":"The body of nine sub-bodies, sustained by the ten vital airs, has at its center the awareness-flame — Self-luminous, marked by consciousness.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 34","title":"","sa":["शतारं शतपत्राढ्यं विकीर्णाम्बुजकर्णिकम् । ।","तत्रार्कचन्द्रवह्नीनामुपर्युपरि चिन्तयेत् ।"],"iast":["śatāraṃ śatapatrāḍhyaṃ vikīrṇāmbujakarṇikam | |","tatrārkacandravahnīnāmuparyupari cintayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"The Lord, in the form of fire-of-the-Self, blazes upward; one should meditate on Him as having the awareness-form of immortality.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 35","title":"","sa":["पद्मस्योद्घाटनं कृत्वा बोधचन्द्राग्निसूर्यकम् । ।","तस्य हृद्बीजमाहृत्य आत्मानं चरते ध्रुवम् ।"],"iast":["padmasyodghāṭanaṃ kṛtvā bodhacandrāgnisūryakam | |","tasya hṛdbījamāhṛtya ātmānaṃ carate dhruvam |"],"words":[],"lit":"[There are] four āsanas to be known: Siddhāsana, Bhadrāsana, Padmāsana, and Siṃhāsana — these are the four āsanas.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 36","title":"","sa":["त्रिस्थानं च त्रिमात्रं च त्रिब्रह्म च त्रयाक्षरम् । ।","त्रिमात्रमर्धमात्रं वा यस्तं वेद स वेदवित् ।"],"iast":["tristhānaṃ ca trimātraṃ ca tribrahma ca trayākṣaram | |","trimātramardhamātraṃ vā yastaṃ veda sa vedavit |"],"words":[],"lit":"Pressing the perineum well with the heel, holding firm by the second hand — the wise should meditate, raising the prāṇa to the brow-center.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 37","title":"","sa":["तैलधारमिवाच्छिन्नदीर्घघण्टानिनादवत् । ।","बिन्दुनादकलातीतं यस्तं वेद स वेदवित् ।"],"iast":["tailadhāramivācchinnadīrghaghaṇṭāninādavat | |","bindunādakalātītaṃ yastaṃ veda sa vedavit |"],"words":[],"lit":"Bringing the prāṇa under control, controlling the mind together — the wise one would dissolve the prāṇa together with the mind in the heart.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 38","title":"","sa":["यथैवत्पलनालेन तोयमाकर्षयेन्नरः । ।","तथैवओत्कर्षयेद्वायुं योगी योगपथे स्थितः ।"],"iast":["yathaivatpalanālena toyamākarṣayennaraḥ | |","tathaivaotkarṣayedvāyuṃ yogī yogapathe sthitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"The fourteen nāḍīs flow from the navel-region — among them are Iḍā, Piṅgalā, Suṣumnā in the middle.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 39","title":"","sa":["अर्धमात्रात्मकं कृत्वा कोशीभूतं तु पङ्कजम् । ।","कर्षयेन्नलमात्रेण भ्रुवोर्मध्ये लयं नयेत् ।"],"iast":["ardhamātrātmakaṃ kṛtvā kośībhūtaṃ tu paṅkajam | |","karṣayennalamātreṇa bhruvormadhye layaṃ nayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Sarasvatī, Vāruṇī, Pūṣā, Hastijihvā, Yaśasvinī — Alambusā, Kuhū, Viśvodarī, Śaṅkhinī, Payasvinī, Gāndhārī — these are the channels.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 40","title":"","sa":["भ्रुवोर्मध्ये ललाटे तु नासिकायास्तु मूलतः । ।","जानीयादमृतं स्थानं तद्ब्रह्मायतनं महत् ।"],"iast":["bhruvormadhye lalāṭe tu nāsikāyāstu mūlataḥ | |","jānīyādamṛtaṃ sthānaṃ tadbrahmāyatanaṃ mahat |"],"words":[],"lit":"Iḍā is on the left, Piṅgalā on the right; Suṣumnā in the middle — Brahmā in Iḍā, Viṣṇu in Piṅgalā, Rudra in Suṣumnā.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 41","title":"","sa":["आसनं प्राणसंरोधः प्रत्याहारश्च धारणा । ।","ध्यानं समाधिरेतानि योगाङ्गानि भवन्ति षट् ।"],"iast":["āsanaṃ prāṇasaṃrodhaḥ pratyāhāraśca dhāraṇā | |","dhyānaṃ samādhiretāni yogāṅgāni bhavanti ṣaṭ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Of nāḍīs, ten are principal — among ten thousand are 72,000 minor nāḍīs in the body.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 42","title":"","sa":["आसनानि च तावन्ति यावन्त्यो जीवजातयः । ।","एतेषानतुलान्भेदान्विजानाति महेश्वरः ।"],"iast":["āsanāni ca tāvanti yāvantyo jīvajātayaḥ | |","eteṣānatulānbhedānvijānāti maheśvaraḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"[The body has] the moon (in Iḍā), the sun (in Piṅgalā), Suṣumnā which devours time — the kuṇḍalinī coiled three and a half times rests there.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 43","title":"","sa":["छिद्रं भद्रं तथा सिंहं पद्मं चेति चतुष्टयम् । ।","आधारं प्रथमं चक्रं स्वाधिष्ठानं द्वितीयकम् ।"],"iast":["chidraṃ bhadraṃ tathā siṃhaṃ padmaṃ ceti catuṣṭayam | |","ādhāraṃ prathamaṃ cakraṃ svādhiṣṭhānaṃ dvitīyakam |"],"words":[],"lit":"The first cakra is the support (Mūlādhāra); the second is Svādhiṣṭhāna; below the navel is the Maṇipūraka; above the navel — Anāhata is in the heart.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 44","title":"","sa":["योनिस्थानं तयोर्मध्ये कामरूपं निगद्यते । ।","आधाराख्ये गुदस्थाने पङ्कजं यच्चतुर्दलम् ।"],"iast":["yonisthānaṃ tayormadhye kāmarūpaṃ nigadyate | |","ādhārākhye gudasthāne paṅkajaṃ yaccaturdalam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Above the throat is the supreme Viśuddhi; between the brows is the Ājñā. The Brahmarandhra and the great place [are at the crown] — thus the six wheels are declared.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 45","title":"","sa":["तन्मध्ये प्रोच्यते योनिः कामाख्या सिद्धवन्दिता । ।","योनिमध्ये स्थितं लिङ्गं पश्चिमाभिमुखं तथा ।"],"iast":["tanmadhye procyate yoniḥ kāmākhyā siddhavanditā | |","yonimadhye sthitaṃ liṅgaṃ paścimābhimukhaṃ tathā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mūlādhāra, Svādhiṣṭhāna, Maṇipūra, Anāhata, Viśuddhi, Ājñā — and the supreme Brahmarandhra.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 46","title":"","sa":["मस्तके मणिवद्भिन्नं यो जानाति स योगवित् । ।","तप्तचामीकराकारं तडिल्लेखेव विस्फुरत् ।"],"iast":["mastake maṇivadbhinnaṃ yo jānāti sa yogavit | |","taptacāmīkarākāraṃ taḍillekheva visphurat |"],"words":[],"lit":"By piercing through these — the yogi crosses saṃsāra — there is no doubt.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 47","title":"","sa":["चतुरस्रमुपर्यग्नेरधो मेढ्रात्प्रतिष्ठितम् । ।","स्वशब्देन भवेत्प्राणः स्वाधिष्ठानं तदाश्रयम् ।"],"iast":["caturasramuparyagneradho meḍhrātpratiṣṭhitam | |","svaśabdena bhavetprāṇaḥ svādhiṣṭhānaṃ tadāśrayam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having seated himself in Padmāsana, of cheerful face, neck-straight, with hands joined on the lap, with eyes [fixed] on the tip of the nose —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 48","title":"","sa":["स्वाधिष्ठानं ततश्चक्रं मेढ्रमेव निगद्यते । ।","मणिवत्तन्तुना यत्र वायुना पूरितं वपुः ।"],"iast":["svādhiṣṭhānaṃ tataścakraṃ meḍhrameva nigadyate | |","maṇivattantunā yatra vāyunā pūritaṃ vapuḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Tongue placed against the upper palate, lips slightly parted — drawing in the breath through Iḍā, holding by kumbhaka with all force —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 49","title":"","sa":["तन्नाभिमण्डलं चक्रं प्रोच्यते मणिपूरकम् । ।","द्वादशारमहाचक्रे पुण्यपापनियन्त्रितः ।"],"iast":["tannābhimaṇḍalaṃ cakraṃ procyate maṇipūrakam | |","dvādaśāramahācakre puṇyapāpaniyantritaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Then exhaling through Piṅgalā — slowly, slowly, by mātrās. Repeating this every day — the yogi becomes accomplished in three months.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"12. Verses 1-50 (Bindu-meditation, OM, nāda) · 50","title":"","sa":["तावज्जीवो भ्रमत्येवं यावत्तत्त्वं न विन्दति । ।","ऊर्ध्वं मेढ्रादथो नाभेः कन्दो योऽस्ति खगाण्डवत् ।"],"iast":["tāvajjīvo bhramatyevaṃ yāvattattvaṃ na vindati | |","ūrdhvaṃ meḍhrādatho nābheḥ kando yo'sti khagāṇḍavat |"],"words":[],"lit":"In the great house of the body, in the empty hollow, the consciousness-form fire — meditate on this with attention, O Knower-of-Brahman.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization)","u":[{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 51","title":"","sa":["तत्र नाड्यः समुत्पन्नाः सहस्राणि द्विसप्ततिः । ।","तेषु नाडीसहस्रेषु द्विसप्ततिरुदाहृताः ।"],"iast":["tatra nāḍyaḥ samutpannāḥ sahasrāṇi dvisaptatiḥ | |","teṣu nāḍīsahasreṣu dvisaptatirudāhṛtāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"In the heart, the lotus of eight petals; below the navel, the lotus of sixteen petals — having brought the mind there, one should meditate on the form of the Lord.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 52","title":"","sa":["प्रधानाः प्राणवाहिन्यो भूयस्तत्र दश स्मृताः । ।","इडा च पिङ्गला चैव सुषुम्ना च तृतीयका ।"],"iast":["pradhānāḥ prāṇavāhinyo bhūyastatra daśa smṛtāḥ | |","iḍā ca piṅgalā caiva suṣumnā ca tṛtīyakā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Within the heart of every being abides the Lord, of the form of OM. He is enjoyer and giver, witness and the deathless.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 53","title":"","sa":["गान्धारी हस्तिजिह्वा च पूषा चैव यशस्विनि । ।","अलम्बुसा कुहूरत्र शङ्खिनी दशमी स्मृता ।"],"iast":["gāndhārī hastijihvā ca pūṣā caiva yaśasvini | |","alambusā kuhūratra śaṅkhinī daśamī smṛtā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Lord of beings, watcher of the world — bestowing fruits of merits and demerits — He is the giver of fearlessness to those who go to Him for refuge.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 54","title":"","sa":["एवं नाडीमयं चक्रं विज्ञेयं योगिना सदा । ।","सततं प्राणवाहिन्यः सोम सूर्याग्निदेवताः ।"],"iast":["evaṃ nāḍīmayaṃ cakraṃ vijñeyaṃ yoginā sadā | |","satataṃ prāṇavāhinyaḥ soma sūryāgnidevatāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Bondage and liberation arise from the mind; the cause of bondage is attachment to objects; liberation arises from detachment from objects — there is no doubt in this.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 55","title":"","sa":["इडापिङ्गलासुषुम्नास्तिस्रो नाड्यः प्रकीर्तिताः । ।","इडा वामे स्थिता भागे पिङ्गला दक्षिणे स्थिता ।"],"iast":["iḍāpiṅgalāsuṣumnāstisro nāḍyaḥ prakīrtitāḥ | |","iḍā vāme sthitā bhāge piṅgalā dakṣiṇe sthitā |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the mind, established in the Self, is dissolved into Brahman — then liberation arises; until then, [the soul] wanders in saṃsāra.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 56","title":"","sa":["सुषुम्ना मध्यदेशे तु प्राणमार्गास्त्रयः स्मृताः । ।","प्राणोऽपानः समानश्चोदानो व्यानस्तथैव च ।"],"iast":["suṣumnā madhyadeśe tu prāṇamārgāstrayaḥ smṛtāḥ | |","prāṇo'pānaḥ samānaścodāno vyānastathaiva ca |"],"words":[],"lit":"The yogi pulls back the senses from objects, like a tortoise its limbs into its shell — establishing the mind in the Self, one should meditate continuously.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 57","title":"","sa":["नागः कूर्मः कृकरको देवदत्तो धनञ्जयः । ।","प्राणाद्याः पञ्च विख्याता नागाद्याः पञ्च वायवः ।"],"iast":["nāgaḥ kūrmaḥ kṛkarako devadatto dhanañjayaḥ | |","prāṇādyāḥ pañca vikhyātā nāgādyāḥ pañca vāyavaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the mind becomes stilled, knowing the Self by the Self — having attained boundless joy in the Self — one is no longer turned back to saṃsāra.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 58","title":"","sa":["एते नाडीसहस्रेषु वर्तन्ते जीवरूपिणः । ।","प्राणापानवशो जीवो ह्यधश्चोर्ध्वं प्रधावति ।"],"iast":["ete nāḍīsahasreṣu vartante jīvarūpiṇaḥ | |","prāṇāpānavaśo jīvo hyadhaścordhvaṃ pradhāvati |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the wise sees the supreme abode of Viṣṇu — like an eye [seeing] the sky spread above — that is what is to be known.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 59","title":"","sa":["वामदक्षिणमार्गेण चञ्चलत्वान्न दृश्यते । ।","आक्षिप्तो भुजदण्डेन यथोच्चलति कन्दुकः ।"],"iast":["vāmadakṣiṇamārgeṇa cañcalatvānna dṛśyate | |","ākṣipto bhujadaṇḍena yathoccalati kandukaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"The fire of yoga blazes in the body — destroying impurities; through that, knowledge arises; from knowledge, liberation; from liberation, the eternal abode.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 60","title":"","sa":["प्राणापानसमाक्षिप्तस्तद्वज्जीवो न विश्रमेत् । ।","अपानात्कर्षति प्राणोऽपानः प्राणाच्च कर्षति ।"],"iast":["prāṇāpānasamākṣiptastadvajjīvo na viśramet | |","apānātkarṣati prāṇo'pānaḥ prāṇācca karṣati |"],"words":[],"lit":"Praṇavavāhinī [Suṣumnā] — of one who is intent on practice — appears as a bright stream from the perineum to the crown.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 61","title":"","sa":["खगरज्जुवदित्येतद्यो जानाति स योगवित् । ।","हकारेण बहिर्याति सकारेण विशेत्पुनः ।"],"iast":["khagarajjuvadityetadyo jānāti sa yogavit | |","hakāreṇa bahiryāti sakāreṇa viśetpunaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Within the suṣumnā, the kuṇḍalinī awakens — like lightning in the cloud — by the power of nāda, established in the brahmarandhra.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 62","title":"","sa":["हंसहंसेत्यमं मन्त्रं जीवो जपति सर्वदा । ।","शतानि षट्दिवारात्रं सहस्राणेकविंशतिः ।"],"iast":["haṃsahaṃsetyamaṃ mantraṃ jīvo japati sarvadā | |","śatāni ṣaṭdivārātraṃ sahasrāṇekaviṃśatiḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"She, the four-armed, conqueror of the world, the all-power — having awakened — pierces the three knots and rises to the supreme abode.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 63","title":"","sa":["एतन्सङ्ख्यान्वितं मन्त्रं जीवो जपति सर्वदा । ।","अजपा नाम गायत्री योगिनां मोक्षदा सदा ।"],"iast":["etansaṅkhyānvitaṃ mantraṃ jīvo japati sarvadā | |","ajapā nāma gāyatrī yogināṃ mokṣadā sadā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Therefore one should always practice yoga; by practice one obtains success — without practice, no accomplishment occurs.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 64","title":"","sa":["अस्याः सङ्कल्पमात्रेण नरः पापैः प्रमुच्यते । ।","अनया सदृशी विद्या अनया सदृशो जपः ।"],"iast":["asyāḥ saṅkalpamātreṇa naraḥ pāpaiḥ pramucyate | |","anayā sadṛśī vidyā anayā sadṛśo japaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mantra-yoga and laya-yoga and haṭha-yoga and rāja-yoga — the foremost yogis declare four kinds of yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 65","title":"","sa":["अनया सदृशं पुण्यं न भूतं न भविष्यति । ।","येन मार्गेण गन्तव्यं ब्रह्मस्थानं निरामयम् ।"],"iast":["anayā sadṛśaṃ puṇyaṃ na bhūtaṃ na bhaviṣyati | |","yena mārgeṇa gantavyaṃ brahmasthānaṃ nirāmayam |"],"words":[],"lit":"By mantra-yoga the mantra is accomplished; by laya-yoga the dissolution of mind; by haṭha-yoga the conquest of the breath; by rāja-yoga the king of yogas.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 66","title":"","sa":["मुखेनाच्छाद्य तद्द्वारं प्रसुप्ता परमेश्वरी । ।","प्रबुद्धा वह्नियोगेन मनसा मरुता सह ।"],"iast":["mukhenācchādya taddvāraṃ prasuptā parameśvarī | |","prabuddhā vahniyogena manasā marutā saha |"],"words":[],"lit":"All four are limbs of the single yoga, leading to the supreme. From breath-restraint comes [steadiness]; from steadiness comes meditation; from meditation, the result.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 67","title":"","sa":["सूचिवद्गुणमादाय व्रजत्यूर्ध्वं सुषुम्नया । ।","उद्घाटयेत्कपाटं तु यथा कुञ्चिकया हठात् ।"],"iast":["sūcivadguṇamādāya vrajatyūrdhvaṃ suṣumnayā | |","udghāṭayetkapāṭaṃ tu yathā kuñcikayā haṭhāt |"],"words":[],"lit":"The yogi should always make the air go to the heart — by restraining it there, the highest state is attained.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 68","title":"","sa":["कुण्डलिन्या तया योगी मोक्षद्वारं विभेदयेत् ।"],"iast":["kuṇḍalinyā tayā yogī mokṣadvāraṃ vibhedayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"Driving the air through Suṣumnā, having dissolved the mind there — the yogi sees the supreme abode — there is no doubt.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 69","title":"","sa":["कृत्वा सम्पुटितौ करौ दृढतरं बध्वाथ पद्मासनम् । ।","गाढं वक्षसि सन्निधाय चुबुकं ध्यानं च तच्चेतसि ॥ ।","वारंवारममपातमूर्ध्वमनिलं प्रोच्चारयन्पूरितम् । ।","मुञ्चन्प्राणमुपैति बोधमतुलं शक्तिप्रभावान्नरः ।"],"iast":["kṛtvā sampuṭitau karau dṛḍhataraṃ badhvātha padmāsanam | |","gāḍhaṃ vakṣasi sannidhāya cubukaṃ dhyānaṃ ca taccetasi || |","vāraṃvāramamapātamūrdhvamanilaṃ proccārayanpūritam | |","muñcanprāṇamupaiti bodhamatulaṃ śaktiprabhāvānnaraḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Khecarī mudrā: by turning the tongue back and entering the cavity above the palate, with the gaze on the brow-middle —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 70","title":"","sa":["पद्मासनस्थितो योगी नाडीद्वारेषु पूरयन् । ।","मारुतं कुम्भयन्यस्तु स मुक्तो नात्र संशयः ।"],"iast":["padmāsanasthito yogī nāḍīdvāreṣu pūrayan | |","mārutaṃ kumbhayanyastu sa mukto nātra saṃśayaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"One should hold [thus] — this is the Khecarī mudrā. By it, sleep, hunger, thirst, and aging are conquered.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 71","title":"","sa":["अङ्गानां मर्दनं कृत्वा श्रमजातेन वारिणा । ।","कट्वम्ललवणत्यागी क्षीरपानरतः सुखी ।"],"iast":["aṅgānāṃ mardanaṃ kṛtvā śramajātena vāriṇā | |","kaṭvamlalavaṇatyāgī kṣīrapānarataḥ sukhī |"],"words":[],"lit":"The yogi, attaining Khecarī-state, is not afflicted by disease, by karma, by saṃsāra — he is freed from all bondage.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 72","title":"","sa":["ब्रह्मचारी मिताहारी योगी योगपरायणः । ।","अब्दादूर्ध्वं भवेत्सिद्धो नात्र कार्यां विचारणा ।"],"iast":["brahmacārī mitāhārī yogī yogaparāyaṇaḥ | |","abdādūrdhvaṃ bhavetsiddho nātra kāryāṃ vicāraṇā |"],"words":[],"lit":"When the prāṇa moves in the void, the mind reaches the void-state; the void-yogi, by establishing the void in the void, attains samādhi.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 73","title":"","sa":["कन्दोर्ध्वकुण्डली शक्तिः स योगी सिद्धिभाजनम् । ।","अपानप्राणयोरैक्यं क्षयन्मूत्रपुरीषयोः ।"],"iast":["kandordhvakuṇḍalī śaktiḥ sa yogī siddhibhājanam | |","apānaprāṇayoraikyaṃ kṣayanmūtrapurīṣayoḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"One should hold the breath everywhere — restraining it in the body — drawing the prāṇa to the brahmarandhra, becoming the all-pervader.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 74","title":"","sa":["युवा भवति वृद्धोऽपि सततं मूलबन्धनात् । ।","पार्ष्णिभागेन सम्पीड्य योनिमाकुञ्चयेद्गुदम् ।"],"iast":["yuvā bhavati vṛddho'pi satataṃ mūlabandhanāt | |","pārṣṇibhāgena sampīḍya yonimākuñcayedgudam |"],"words":[],"lit":"The kuṇḍalinī, by the practice of khecarī-mudrā, becomes awakened — rising through the suṣumnā, she reaches the abode of Śiva.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 75","title":"","sa":["अपानमूर्ध्वमुत्कृष्य मूलबन्धोऽयमुच्यते । ।","उड्याणं कुरुते यस्मादविश्रान्तमहाखगः ।"],"iast":["apānamūrdhvamutkṛṣya mūlabandho'yamucyate | |","uḍyāṇaṃ kurute yasmādaviśrāntamahākhagaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"There, in the thousand-petaled lotus, Śiva and Śakti unite — and from that union, the practitioner attains supreme bliss.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 76","title":"","sa":["उड्डियाणं तदेव स्यात्तत्र बन्धो विधीयते । ।","उदरे पश्चिमं ताणं नाभेरूर्ध्वं तु कारयेत् ।"],"iast":["uḍḍiyāṇaṃ tadeva syāttatra bandho vidhīyate | |","udare paścimaṃ tāṇaṃ nābherūrdhvaṃ tu kārayet |"],"words":[],"lit":"The nectar that flows from the union of Śiva and Śakti in the brahmarandhra — by drinking this with the tongue-tip pressed up — the yogi conquers death.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 77","title":"","sa":["उड्डियाणोऽप्ययं बन्धो मृत्युमातङ्गकेसरी । ।","बध्नाति हि शिरोजातमधोगामिनभोजलम् ।"],"iast":["uḍḍiyāṇo'pyayaṃ bandho mṛtyumātaṅgakesarī | |","badhnāti hi śirojātamadhogāminabhojalam |"],"words":[],"lit":"Indeed, by this mudrā the yogi conquers death — there is no doubt. The body becomes radiant; no diseases arise.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 78","title":"","sa":["ततो जालन्धरो बन्धः कर्मदुःखौघनाशनः । ।","जालन्धरे कृते बन्धे कर्णसंकोचलक्षणे ।"],"iast":["tato jālandharo bandhaḥ karmaduḥkhaughanāśanaḥ | |","jālandhare kṛte bandhe karṇasaṃkocalakṣaṇe |"],"words":[],"lit":"He sees what is far, hears what is far — by perfection in this mudrā. All powers (siddhis) arise to him as servants.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 79","title":"","sa":["न पीयूषं पतत्यग्नौ न च वायुः प्रधावति । ।","कपालकुहरे जिह्वा प्रविष्टा विपरीतगा ।"],"iast":["na pīyūṣaṃ patatyagnau na ca vāyuḥ pradhāvati | |","kapālakuhare jihvā praviṣṭā viparītagā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Therefore the yogi should practice Khecarī-mudrā with all effort — by perfection in it, all yogic perfections arise.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 80","title":"","sa":["भ्रुवोरन्तर्गता दृष्टिर्मुद्रा भवति खेचरी । ।","न रोगो मरणं तस्य न निद्रा न क्षुधा तृषा ।"],"iast":["bhruvorantargatā dṛṣṭirmudrā bhavati khecarī | |","na rogo maraṇaṃ tasya na nidrā na kṣudhā tṛṣā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mahāmudrā, mahābandha, and the triple mahāvedha — the three locks — Jālandhara, Uḍḍiyāna, Mūlabandha — these are the supreme mudrās.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 81","title":"","sa":["न च मूर्च्छा भवेत्तस्य यो मुद्रां वेत्ति खेचरीम् । ।","पीड्यते न च रोगेण लिप्यते न च कर्मणा ।"],"iast":["na ca mūrcchā bhavettasya yo mudrāṃ vetti khecarīm | |","pīḍyate na ca rogeṇa lipyate na ca karmaṇā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mahāmudrā: with left heel pressed against the perineum, the right leg extended, hands holding the toe firmly —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 82","title":"","sa":["बध्यते न च कालेन यस्य मुद्रस्ति खेचरी । ।","चित्तं चरति खे यस्माज्जिह्वा भवति खेगता ।"],"iast":["badhyate na ca kālena yasya mudrasti khecarī | |","cittaṃ carati khe yasmājjihvā bhavati khegatā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Drawing the breath inward, with chin pressed to the chest — holding by kumbhaka — then releasing slowly — this is Mahāmudrā.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 83","title":"","sa":["तेनैषा खेचरी नाम मुद्रा सिद्धनमस्कृता । ।","खेचर्या मुद्रया यस्य विवरं लम्बिकोर्ध्वतः ।"],"iast":["tenaiṣā khecarī nāma mudrā siddhanamaskṛtā | |","khecaryā mudrayā yasya vivaraṃ lambikordhvataḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"By practicing it on both sides — the yogi becomes free from all diseases — the digestive fire is increased — the body becomes strong.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 84","title":"","sa":["बिन्दुः क्षरति नो यस्य कामिन्यालिङ्गितस्य च । ।","यावद्बिन्दुः स्थितो देहे तावन्मृत्युभयं कुतः ।"],"iast":["binduḥ kṣarati no yasya kāminyāliṅgitasya ca | |","yāvadbinduḥ sthito dehe tāvanmṛtyubhayaṃ kutaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mahābandha: extending the right leg, placing the left heel on the right thigh — by the same procedure as Mahāmudrā.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 85","title":"","sa":["यावद्बद्धा नभोमुद्रा तावद्बिन्दुर्न गच्छति । ।","गलितोऽपि यदा बिन्दुः सम्प्राप्तो योनिमण्डले ।"],"iast":["yāvadbaddhā nabhomudrā tāvadbindurna gacchati | |","galito'pi yadā binduḥ samprāpto yonimaṇḍale |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mahāvedha: from Mahābandha, beating the buttocks against the seat — by which the prāṇa is forced into Suṣumnā.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 86","title":"","sa":["व्रजत्यूर्ध्वं हठाच्छक्त्या निबद्धो योनिमुद्रया । ।","स एव द्विविधो बिन्दुः पाण्डरो लोहितस्तथा ।"],"iast":["vrajatyūrdhvaṃ haṭhācchaktyā nibaddho yonimudrayā | |","sa eva dvividho binduḥ pāṇḍaro lohitastathā |"],"words":[],"lit":"Jālandhara-bandha: contracting the throat firmly, pressing the chin to the heart — the bandha by which prāṇa is restrained at the throat.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 87","title":"","sa":["पाण्डरं शुक्रमित्याहुर्लोहिताख्यं महारजः । ।","विद्रुमद्रुमसंकाशं योनिस्थाने स्थितं रजः ।"],"iast":["pāṇḍaraṃ śukramityāhurlohitākhyaṃ mahārajaḥ | |","vidrumadrumasaṃkāśaṃ yonisthāne sthitaṃ rajaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Uḍḍiyāna-bandha: drawing the abdomen up under the ribs — by which prāṇa rises through Suṣumnā — the lion against the elephant of death.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 88","title":"","sa":["शशिस्थाने वसेद्बिन्दुःस्तयोरैक्यं सुदुर्लभम् । ।","बिन्दुः शिवो रजः शक्तिर्बिन्दुरिन्दू रजो रविः ।"],"iast":["śaśisthāne vasedbinduḥstayoraikyaṃ sudurlabham | |","binduḥ śivo rajaḥ śaktirbindurindū rajo raviḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Mūla-bandha: contracting the perineum forcefully — drawing the apāna upward — uniting prāṇa and apāna at the navel.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 89","title":"","sa":["उभयओः सङ्गमादेव प्राप्यते परमं वपुः । ।","वायुना शक्तिचालेन प्रेरितं खे यथा रजः ।"],"iast":["ubhayaoḥ saṅgamādeva prāpyate paramaṃ vapuḥ | |","vāyunā śakticālena preritaṃ khe yathā rajaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"By the union of prāṇa and apāna through Mūla-bandha — the nāda-bindu attain unity — the yogi attains the perfection of yoga.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 90","title":"","sa":["रविणैकत्वमायाति भवेद्दिव्यं वपुस्तदा । ।","शुक्लं चन्द्रेण संयुक्तं रजः सूर्यसमन्वितम् ।"],"iast":["raviṇaikatvamāyāti bhaveddivyaṃ vapustadā | |","śuklaṃ candreṇa saṃyuktaṃ rajaḥ sūryasamanvitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"These nine — the four āsanas, the three locks, the great mudrā, and Khecarī — are the secret means of yogic accomplishment.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 91","title":"","sa":["द्वयोः समरसीभावं यो जानाति स योगवित् । ।","शोधनं मलजालानां घटनं चन्द्रसूर्ययोः ।"],"iast":["dvayoḥ samarasībhāvaṃ yo jānāti sa yogavit | |","śodhanaṃ malajālānāṃ ghaṭanaṃ candrasūryayoḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"By long practice, the yogi sees the inner light — first like a firefly, then like a lamp, then like the sun.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 92","title":"","sa":["रसानां शोषणं सम्यङ्महामुद्राभिधीयते ।"],"iast":["rasānāṃ śoṣaṇaṃ samyaṅmahāmudrābhidhīyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"The body becomes light, radiant; the senses become refined — speech becomes prophetic; the past and future are known.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 93","title":"","sa":["वक्षोन्यस्तहनुर्निपीड्य सुषिरं योनेश्च वामाङ्घ्रिणा ।","हस्ताभ्यामनुधारयन्प्रविततं पादं तथा दक्षिणम् ॥ ।","आपूर्य श्वसनेन कुक्षियुगलं बध्वा शनैरेचये\\- ।","देषा पातकनाशिनी ननु महामुद्रा नृणां प्रोच्यते ।"],"iast":["vakṣonyastahanurnipīḍya suṣiraṃ yoneśca vāmāṅghriṇā |","hastābhyāmanudhārayanpravitataṃ pādaṃ tathā dakṣiṇam || |","āpūrya śvasanena kukṣiyugalaṃ badhvā śanairecaye\\- |","deṣā pātakanāśinī nanu mahāmudrā nṛṇāṃ procyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"He moves as he pleases — sky-walking, water-walking — invisible at will — all powers of the masters of yoga arise.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 94","title":"","sa":["अथात्मनिर्णयं व्याख्यास्ये ॥ ।","हृदिस्थाने अष्टदलपद्मं वर्तते तन्मध्ये रेखावलयं ।","कृत्वा जीवात्मरूपं ज्योतीरूपमणुमात्रं वर्तते तस्मिन्सर्वं ।","प्रतिष्ठितं भवति सर्वं जानाति सर्वं करोति सर्वमेतच्चरितमहं ।","कर्ताऽहं भोक्ता सुखी दुःखी काणः खञ्जो बधिरो मूकः कृशः ।","स्थूलोऽनेन प्रकारेण स्वतन्त्रवादेन वर्तते ॥ ।","पूर्वदले विश्रमते पूर्वं दलं श्वेतवर्णं तदा भक्तिपुरःसरं ।","धर्मे मतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदाऽग्नेयदले विश्रमते तदाग्नेयदलं रक्तवर्णं तदा निद्रालस्य ।","मतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदा दक्षिणदले विश्रमते तद्दक्षिणदलं कृष्णवर्णं तदा ।","द्वेषकोपमतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदा नैरृतदले विश्रमते तन्नैरृतदलं नीलवर्णं तदा ।","पापकर्महिंसामतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदा पश्चिमदले विश्रमते तत्पश्चिमदलं स्फटिकवर्णं तदा ।","क्रीडाविनोदे मतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदा वायव्यदले विश्रमते वायव्यदलं माणिक्यवर्णं तदा ।","गमनचलनवैराग्यमतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदोत्तरदले विश्रमते तदुत्तरदलं पीतवर्णं तदा सुखशृङ्गार\\- ।","मतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदेशानदले विश्रमते तदीशानदलं वैडूर्यवर्णं तदा ।","दानादिकृपामतिर्भवति ॥ ।","यदा सन्धिसन्धिषु मतिर्भवति तदा वातपित्तश्लेष्ममहाव्याधि\\- ।","प्रकोपो भवति ॥ ।","यदा मध्ये तिष्ठति तदा सर्वं जानाति गायति नृत्यति पठत्यानन्दं ।","करोति ॥ ।","यदा नेत्रश्रमो भवति श्रमनिर्भरणार्थं प्रथमरेखावलयं ।","कृत्वा मध्ये निमज्जनं कुरुते प्रथमरेखाबन्धूकपुष्पवर्णं ।","तदा निद्रावस्था भवति ॥ ।","निद्रावस्थामध्ये स्वप्नावस्था भवति ॥ ।","स्वप्नावस्थामध्ये दृष्टं श्रुतमनुमानसम्भववार्ता ।","इत्यादिकल्पनां करोति तदादिश्रमो भवति ॥ ।","श्रमनिर्हरणार्थं द्वितीयरेखावलयं कृत्वा मध्ये ।","निमज्जनं कुरुते द्वितीयरेखा इन्द्रकोपवर्णं तदा ।","सुषुप्त्यवस्था भवति सुषुप्तौ केवलपरमेश्वरसम्बन्धिनी ।","बुद्दिर्भवति नित्यबोधस्वरूपा भवति पश्चात्परमेश्वर\\- ।","स्वरूपेण प्राप्तिर्भवति ॥ ।","तृतीयरेखावलयं कृत्वा मध्ये निमज्जनं कुरुते तृतीयरेखा ।","पद्मरागवर्णं तदा तुरीयावस्था भवति तुरीये केवलपरमात्म\\- ।","सम्बन्धिनी भवति नित्यबोधस्वरूपा भवति तदा शनैः ।","शनैरुपरमेद्बुद्ध्या धृतिगृहीतयात्मसंस्थं मनः ।","कृत्वा न किञ्चिदपि चिन्तयेत्तदा प्राणापानयोरैक्यं कृत्वा ।","सर्वं विश्वमात्मस्वरूपेण लक्ष्यं धारयति । यदा ।","तुरीयातीतावस्था तदा सर्वेषामानन्दस्वरूपो भवति ।","द्वन्द्वातीतो भवति यावद्देहधारणा वर्तते तावत्तिष्ठति ।","पश्चात्परमात्मस्वरूपेण प्राप्तिर्भवति इत्यनेन प्रकारेण ।","मोक्षो भवतीदमेवात्मदर्शनोपायं भवन्ति ॥ ।","चतुष्पथसमायुक्तमहाद्वारगवायुना । ।","सह स्थितत्रिकोणार्धगमने दृश्यतेऽच्युतः ।"],"iast":["athātmanirṇayaṃ vyākhyāsye || |","hṛdisthāne aṣṭadalapadmaṃ vartate tanmadhye rekhāvalayaṃ |","kṛtvā jīvātmarūpaṃ jyotīrūpamaṇumātraṃ vartate tasminsarvaṃ |","pratiṣṭhitaṃ bhavati sarvaṃ jānāti sarvaṃ karoti sarvametaccaritamahaṃ |","kartā'haṃ bhoktā sukhī duḥkhī kāṇaḥ khañjo badhiro mūkaḥ kṛśaḥ |","sthūlo'nena prakāreṇa svatantravādena vartate || |","pūrvadale viśramate pūrvaṃ dalaṃ śvetavarṇaṃ tadā bhaktipuraḥsaraṃ |","dharme matirbhavati || |","yadā'gneyadale viśramate tadāgneyadalaṃ raktavarṇaṃ tadā nidrālasya |","matirbhavati || |","yadā dakṣiṇadale viśramate taddakṣiṇadalaṃ kṛṣṇavarṇaṃ tadā |","dveṣakopamatirbhavati || |","yadā nairṛtadale viśramate tannairṛtadalaṃ nīlavarṇaṃ tadā |","pāpakarmahiṃsāmatirbhavati || |","yadā paścimadale viśramate tatpaścimadalaṃ sphaṭikavarṇaṃ tadā |","krīḍāvinode matirbhavati || |","yadā vāyavyadale viśramate vāyavyadalaṃ māṇikyavarṇaṃ tadā |","gamanacalanavairāgyamatirbhavati || |","yadottaradale viśramate taduttaradalaṃ pītavarṇaṃ tadā sukhaśṛṅgāra\\- |","matirbhavati || |","yadeśānadale viśramate tadīśānadalaṃ vaiḍūryavarṇaṃ tadā |","dānādikṛpāmatirbhavati || |","yadā sandhisandhiṣu matirbhavati tadā vātapittaśleṣmamahāvyādhi\\- |","prakopo bhavati || |","yadā madhye tiṣṭhati tadā sarvaṃ jānāti gāyati nṛtyati paṭhatyānandaṃ |","karoti || |","yadā netraśramo bhavati śramanirbharaṇārthaṃ prathamarekhāvalayaṃ |","kṛtvā madhye nimajjanaṃ kurute prathamarekhābandhūkapuṣpavarṇaṃ |","tadā nidrāvasthā bhavati || |","nidrāvasthāmadhye svapnāvasthā bhavati || |","svapnāvasthāmadhye dṛṣṭaṃ śrutamanumānasambhavavārtā |","ityādikalpanāṃ karoti tadādiśramo bhavati || |","śramanirharaṇārthaṃ dvitīyarekhāvalayaṃ kṛtvā madhye |","nimajjanaṃ kurute dvitīyarekhā indrakopavarṇaṃ tadā |","suṣuptyavasthā bhavati suṣuptau kevalaparameśvarasambandhinī |","buddirbhavati nityabodhasvarūpā bhavati paścātparameśvara\\- |","svarūpeṇa prāptirbhavati || |","tṛtīyarekhāvalayaṃ kṛtvā madhye nimajjanaṃ kurute tṛtīyarekhā |","padmarāgavarṇaṃ tadā turīyāvasthā bhavati turīye kevalaparamātma\\- |","sambandhinī bhavati nityabodhasvarūpā bhavati tadā śanaiḥ |","śanairuparamedbuddhyā dhṛtigṛhītayātmasaṃsthaṃ manaḥ |","kṛtvā na kiñcidapi cintayettadā prāṇāpānayoraikyaṃ kṛtvā |","sarvaṃ viśvamātmasvarūpeṇa lakṣyaṃ dhārayati | yadā |","turīyātītāvasthā tadā sarveṣāmānandasvarūpo bhavati |","dvandvātīto bhavati yāvaddehadhāraṇā vartate tāvattiṣṭhati |","paścātparamātmasvarūpeṇa prāptirbhavati ityanena prakāreṇa |","mokṣo bhavatīdamevātmadarśanopāyaṃ bhavanti || |","catuṣpathasamāyuktamahādvāragavāyunā | |","saha sthitatrikoṇārdhagamane dṛśyate'cyutaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"But the wise yogi does not display these powers — concealing them, he proceeds toward the supreme goal alone.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 95","title":"","sa":["पूर्वोक्तत्रिकोणस्थानादुपरि पृथिव्यादिपञ्चवर्णकं ध्येयम् । ।","प्राणादिपञ्चवायुश्च बीजं वर्णं च स्थानकम् । ।","यकारं प्राणबीजं च नीलजीमूतसन्निभम् । ।","रकारमग्निबीजं च अपानादित्यसंनिभम् ।"],"iast":["pūrvoktatrikoṇasthānādupari pṛthivyādipañcavarṇakaṃ dhyeyam | |","prāṇādipañcavāyuśca bījaṃ varṇaṃ ca sthānakam | |","yakāraṃ prāṇabījaṃ ca nīlajīmūtasannibham | |","rakāramagnibījaṃ ca apānādityasaṃnibham |"],"words":[],"lit":"Having pierced through all the cakras, having drunk the nectar of immortality — the yogi sees the supreme light in the brahmarandhra.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 96","title":"","sa":["लकारं पृथिवीरूपं व्यानं बन्धूकसंनिभम् । ।","वकारं जीवबीजं च उदानं शङ्खवर्णकम् ।"],"iast":["lakāraṃ pṛthivīrūpaṃ vyānaṃ bandhūkasaṃnibham | |","vakāraṃ jīvabījaṃ ca udānaṃ śaṅkhavarṇakam |"],"words":[],"lit":"There, his Self appears — like the sun in the sky — radiant, indescribable, the highest state — the eternal bliss.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 97","title":"","sa":["हकारं वियत्स्वरूपं च समानं स्फटिकप्रभम् । ।","हृन्नाभिनासाकर्णं च पादाङ्गुष्ठादिसंस्थितम् ।"],"iast":["hakāraṃ viyatsvarūpaṃ ca samānaṃ sphaṭikaprabham | |","hṛnnābhināsākarṇaṃ ca pādāṅguṣṭhādisaṃsthitam |"],"words":[],"lit":"This is the abode of Viṣṇu, of Śiva, of Brahman — the place beyond all change — by knowing this, one becomes immortal.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 98","title":"","sa":["द्विसप्ततिसहस्राणि नाडीमार्गेषु वर्तते । ।","अष्टाविंशतिकोटीषु रोमकूपेषु संस्थिताः ।"],"iast":["dvisaptatisahasrāṇi nāḍīmārgeṣu vartate | |","aṣṭāviṃśatikoṭīṣu romakūpeṣu saṃsthitāḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"Eka-prāṇa indeed — the Self alone — Jīva is one alone — by practicing the threefold recaka, pūraka, kumbhaka with firm mind and concentration —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 99","title":"","sa":["समानप्राण एकस्तु जीवः स एक एव हि । ।","रेचकादि त्रयं कुर्याद्दृढचित्तः समाहितः ।"],"iast":["samānaprāṇa ekastu jīvaḥ sa eka eva hi | |","recakādi trayaṃ kuryāddṛḍhacittaḥ samāhitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"[The yogi obtains the goal.] Slowly drawing everything [in], in the cavity of the heart-lotus — binding prāṇa and apāna — one should utter the praṇava.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 100","title":"","sa":["शनैः समस्तमाकृष्य हृत्सरोरुहकोटरे । ।","प्राणापानौ च बध्वा तु प्रणवेन समुच्चरेत् ।"],"iast":["śanaiḥ samastamākṛṣya hṛtsaroruhakoṭare | |","prāṇāpānau ca badhvā tu praṇavena samuccaret |"],"words":[],"lit":"Contracting the ears, contracting the genitals — from Mūlādhāra, Suṣumnā shines like a lotus-fiber, auspicious.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 101","title":"","sa":["कर्णसङ्कोचनं कृत्वा लिङ्गसङ्कोचनं तथा । ।","मूलाधारात्सुषुम्ना च पद्मतन्तुनिभा शुभा ।"],"iast":["karṇasaṅkocanaṃ kṛtvā liṅgasaṅkocanaṃ tathā | |","mūlādhārātsuṣumnā ca padmatantunibhā śubhā |"],"words":[],"lit":"The formless nāda arises — like the sound from a vīṇā-stick — like the shell-sound and the like — in the middle, the resonance flows.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 102","title":"","sa":["अमूर्तो वर्तते नादो वीणादण्डसमुत्थितः । ।","शङ्खनादिभिश्चैव मध्यमेव ध्वनिर्यथा ।"],"iast":["amūrto vartate nādo vīṇādaṇḍasamutthitaḥ | |","śaṅkhanādibhiścaiva madhyameva dhvaniryathā |"],"words":[],"lit":"[The yogi hears] the nāda gone to the void-aperture, the peacock-sound also — in the middle of the cranium-cavity, in the middle of the four-doored [space].","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 103","title":"","sa":["व्योमरन्ध्रगतो नादो मायूरं नादमेव च । ।","कपालकुहरे मध्ये चतुर्द्वारस्य मध्यमे ।"],"iast":["vyomarandhragato nādo māyūraṃ nādameva ca | |","kapālakuhare madhye caturdvārasya madhyame |"],"words":[],"lit":"There the Self shines forth — like the sun in the sky. Between the two bows (eyebrows), in the brahma-randhras, by [śakti's] power —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 104","title":"","sa":["तदात्मा राजते तत्र यथा व्योम्नि दिवाकरः । ।","कोदण्डद्वयमध्ये तु ब्रह्मरन्ध्रेषु शक्तितः ।"],"iast":["tadātmā rājate tatra yathā vyomni divākaraḥ | |","kodaṇḍadvayamadhye tu brahmarandhreṣu śaktitaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"One sees his own Self, the Person — there the mind has gone to dissolution. The jewels of light, of moonlight-sound, the bindu, the Maheśvara-state —","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"13. Verses 51-105 (Cakras, mudrās, kuṇḍalinī, final realization) · 105","title":"","sa":["स्वात्मानं पुरुषं पश्येन्मनस्तत्र लयं गतम् । ।","रत्नानि ज्योत्स्निनादं तु बिन्दुमाहेश्वरं पदम् । ।","य एवं वेद पुरुषः स कैवल्यं समश्नुत इत्युपनिषत् ।"],"iast":["svātmānaṃ puruṣaṃ paśyenmanastatra layaṃ gatam | |","ratnāni jyotsninādaṃ tu bindumāheśvaraṃ padam | |","ya evaṃ veda puruṣaḥ sa kaivalyaṃ samaśnuta ityupaniṣat |"],"words":[],"lit":"He who knows thus, the Person — that one attains kaivalya — thus says the Upaniṣad.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"14. Closing Śānti Mantra","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु ॥ सह नौ भुनक्तु ॥ सह वीर्यं करवावहै ॥","तेजस्विनावधीतमस्तु मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥","इति ध्यानबिन्दूपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["oṃ saha nāvavatu || saha nau bhunaktu || saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai ||","tejasvināvadhītam astu mā vidviṣāvahai ||","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||","iti dhyāna-bindūpaniṣat samāptā ||  —  \"Thus ends the Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad.\""],"words":[],"lit":"\"OM. May He protect us both; may He nourish us both; may we both work with great vigor; may our learning be brilliant; may we not hate. OM. Peace. Peace. Peace.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"The Top 5 Commentaries","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The five primary commentaries and editions on the Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad","Note: The Yoga Upaniṣads — unlike the 10-13 Mukhya Upaniṣads — do NOT have classical Sanskrit commentaries by Śaṅkara, Rāmānuja, Madhva, or other principal Vedānta ācāryas. The major Vedānta thinkers focused on the principal Upaniṣads. The Yoga Upaniṣads received their main commentarial treatment from later scholars and editors."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"15. #1","title":"Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin (Dīpikā)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["AUTHOR: Saccidānanda Sarasvatī, popularly known as Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin or Brahmayogin Saccidānanda (18th century Tamil Nadu).","WORK: He wrote Sanskrit commentaries (called Dīpikā — \"lamp\") on ALL 108 Muktikā Upaniṣads, including each of the 20 Yoga Upaniṣads. For the Dhyāna Bindu specifically, his Dīpikā is THE classical Sanskrit commentary.","STATUS: Primary traditional commentary. Saccidānanda Sarasvatī's comprehensive 108-Upaniṣad commentarial project is unique in Sanskrit literature — no other classical author commented on the entire Muktikā corpus.","AVAILABILITY: Published as part of the Adyar Library editions of the Yoga Upaniṣads. His Dīpikā is printed alongside the Sanskrit text in:","The Yoga Upaniṣads (Adyar Library Series, 1938, edited by Pandit A. Mahadeva Sastri)","Revised edition by A.G. Krishna Warrier (1952)","CHARACTER: His Dīpikā is Vedāntic in orientation. He reads the Yoga Upaniṣads through the lens of Advaita Vedānta — the yogic practices are understood as means to the realization of \"I am Brahman.\"","The five analogies (vv. 5-7) as illustrations of vyāpti (pervasion) of Brahman in all beings","The OM-analysis (vv. 9-22) as a tantric-Vedāntic synthesis of cosmic correspondences","The cakras (vv. 43-46) as functional centers, not literal anatomical structures","The Khecarī-mudrā (vv. 68-79) as a yogic technique for samādhi-attainment, not magical"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"16. #2","title":"K. Narayanasvami Aiyar (Thirty Minor Upaniṣads, 1914)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["AUTHOR: K. Narayanasvami Aiyar (1846-1932), Tamil scholar of Sanskrit and Theosophy.","WORK: Thirty Minor Upaniṣads — Translated from the Original Sanskrit. Published 1914 in Madras (Chennai). Public domain. Available on archive.org.","STATUS: First complete English translation of the Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad. Until the 1938 Adyar Library edition, this was the only English source for the text.","CHARACTER: Translation with extensive footnotes drawing on Theosophical interpretation. Aiyar was associated with the Theosophical Society and his translations emphasize the meditative-spiritual dimension over strict philology.","FOR DHYĀNA BINDU: Aiyar provides:","Verse-by-verse English translation","Sanskrit text in Roman transliteration","Footnotes explaining technical terms (cakra, nāḍī, mudrā)","Cross-references to other Yoga Upaniṣads","LIMITATIONS: Older translation; some technical terms are rendered ambiguously by modern standards. The Theosophical framing sometimes overlays anachronistic interpretations."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"17. #3","title":"T.R. Srinivasa Ayyangar / A.G. Krishna Warrier (1938 / rev. 1952)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["AUTHOR: T.R. Srinivasa Ayyangar (original 1938) and A.G. Krishna Warrier (revised 1952). Both senior scholars at the Adyar Library.","WORK: The Yoga Upaniṣads (Adyar Library Series). Sanskrit text in Devanāgarī + Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin's Sanskrit Dīpikā + English translation + introduction. The standard scholarly reference for all 20 Yoga Upaniṣads.","STATUS: The most authoritative scholarly edition available. Where Aiyar gives only English translation, Ayyangar/Warrier provides the full critical apparatus: Sanskrit text, Sanskrit commentary, English translation, and notes.","CHARACTER:","Philologically careful — the Sanskrit text is checked against multiple manuscripts","Includes the complete Dīpikā commentary by Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin","Comparative notes connecting Yoga Upaniṣad passages to similar passages in the Haṭha-Yoga-Pradīpikā and Tantric texts","General introduction places the Yoga Upaniṣads in the broader yoga tradition","FOR DHYĀNA BINDU: This edition is the indispensable scholarly companion. The Sanskrit text is reliable, the Dīpikā commentary is included, and the English translation is more literal than Aiyar's."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"18. #4","title":"Swāmī Sivānanda Saraswatī","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["AUTHOR: Swāmī Sivānanda Saraswatī (1887-1963), founder of the Divine Life Society at Rishikesh.","WORK: Selective commentary on the Yoga Upaniṣads in various publications of the Divine Life Society, including books such as Practice of Yoga, Kundalini Yoga, and Concentration and Meditation. Not a single volume on Dhyāna Bindu but distributed treatment.","STATUS: Practical-tradition commentary. Where the Adyar editions are scholarly, Sivānanda's treatment is practitioner-focused.","CHARACTER:","Emphasizes the practical-yogic dimension over the philological","Connects Dhyāna Bindu material to actual practice (japa, prāṇāyāma, dhyāna sequences)","Integrates with Sivānanda's broader Yoga of Synthesis approach","Accessible to practitioners, not specialists","FOR DHYĀNA BINDU SPECIFICALLY: His treatment of the Khecarī-mudrā passages, the cakra-piercing process, and the OM-meditation instructions is widely used in the Sivananda Yoga lineage as practical instruction."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"19. #5","title":"Modern Academic Scholarship","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["SCHOLARS:","Christian Bouy — French scholar; produced detailed studies of the Yoga Upaniṣad corpus and its relationship to Haṭha-Yoga texts in the 1990s","Patrick Olivelle — covered the Yoga Upaniṣads briefly in his comprehensive Upaniṣad surveys, though he did not produce a critical edition of them specifically","K.A. Subramania Iyer — earlier scholar of Indian linguistic philosophy who touched on the Yoga Upaniṣads in his work","Various Indian academic journals — articles in the Adyar Library Bulletin, Journal of the American Oriental Society, etc., on specific passages","STATUS: No comprehensive modern Western critical edition of the Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad has been produced. The text remains within the Indian commentarial tradition (Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin) supplemented by the early 20th-century English translations (Aiyar, Ayyangar/Warrier).","CHARACTER:","Comparative — places Dhyāna Bindu in relation to other Yoga Upaniṣads, Haṭha-Yoga texts, Tantric literature","Critical-historical — questions of dating, recension, textual evolution","Specialized — focused on specific themes (the cakra system, the kuṇḍalinī image, etc.)","FUTURE DIRECTIONS: A critical edition of the Yoga Upaniṣads (parallel to Olivelle's critical edition of the principal Upaniṣads) is a desideratum. Until then, the Adyar Library edition with Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin's Dīpikā remains the authoritative source."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"20. Glossary","title":"","sa":[],"words":[["Dhyāna","ध्यान","Meditation"],["Bindu","बिन्दु","Point / drop / dot"],["Dhyāna-Bindu","ध्यानबिन्दु","The point of meditation"],["Praṇava","प्रणव","OM — the supreme syllable"],["Bīja","बीज","Seed (syllable)"],["Nāda","नाद","Subtle sound"],["Anāhata","अनाहत","Unstruck (sound)"],["Akṣara","अक्षर","Imperishable / syllable"],["Ekākṣara","एकाक्षर","One-syllabled / the imperishable-one"],["Vyāhṛti","व्याहृति","Sacred utterance (bhūr-bhuvaḥ-svaḥ)"],["Pūraka","पूरक","Inhalation"],["Kumbhaka","कुम्भक","Retention"],["Recaka","रेचक","Exhalation"],["Iḍā","इडा","Left nāḍī (moon)"],["Piṅgalā","पिङ्गला","Right nāḍī (sun)"],["Suṣumnā","सुषुम्ना","Central nāḍī"],["Kuṇḍalinī","कुण्डलिनी","Coiled cosmic power at Mūlādhāra"],["Cakra","चक्र","Wheel / energy-center"],["Mūlādhāra","मूलाधार","Foundation cakra (perineum)"],["Svādhiṣṭhāna","स्वाधिष्ठान","Lower-abdomen cakra"],["Maṇipūra(ka)","मणिपूरक","Navel cakra"],["Anāhata","अनाहत","Heart cakra"],["Viśuddhi","विशुद्धि","Throat cakra"],["Ājñā","आज्ञा","Brow cakra"],["Brahmarandhra","ब्रह्मरन्ध्र","Crown opening / supreme abode"],["Khecarī","खेचरी","Sky-walker (mudrā)"],["Mudrā","मुद्रा","Seal / gesture"],["Jālandhara-bandha","जालन्धरबन्ध","Throat lock"],["Uḍḍiyāna-bandha","उड्डियानबन्ध","Abdominal lock"],["Mūla-bandha","मूलबन्ध","Root lock"],["Mahāmudrā","महामुद्रा","Great seal"],["Mahābandha","महाबन्ध","Great lock"],["Mahāvedha","महावेध","Great piercer"],["Araṇi","अरणि","Lower fire-stick (of fire-churning)"],["Vyāpti","व्याप्ति","Pervasion"],["Kaivalya","कैवल्य","Aloneness / supreme isolation / liberation"],["Aṅguṣṭha-mātra","अङ्गुष्ठमात्र","Thumb-sized"],["Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin","—","Saccidānanda Sarasvatī, 18th c. commentator on all 108 Muktikā Upaniṣads"],["Dīpikā","दीपिका","\"Lamp\" — title of Brahmayogin's commentary"]],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"21. Editions and Sources","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Sanskrit text used in this document:","Source: Sanskrit Documents Project (sanskritdocuments.org)","File: dhyanabindu.itx — encoded and proofread by Sunder Hattangadi (Feb 2000)","Description: 39/108 in the Muktikā Upaniṣads; Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda; Yoga Upaniṣad","Primary editions:","The Yoga Upaniṣads (Adyar Library, 1938; revised by A.G. Krishna Warrier, 1952) — with Sanskrit text + Upaniṣad-Brahmayogin's Dīpikā + English translation","K. Narayanasvami Aiyar, Thirty Minor Upaniṣads (Madras, 1914) — first English translation; archive.org","Swāmī Sivānanda — Selected commentary in Divine Life Society publications","Related Bindu Upaniṣads (for comparative study):","Tejo Bindu Upaniṣad — light-meditation + sustained Advaita (462 verses)","Nāda Bindu Upaniṣad — sound-meditation","Amṛta Bindu Upaniṣad — mind's twofold nature (shortest)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"22. Closing","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Dhyāna Bindu Upaniṣad is the most practical of the four Bindu Upaniṣads — a compact 105-verse manual integrating the praṇava-yoga, nāda-yoga, cakra-yoga, and kuṇḍalinī-yoga in a single text. Its opening claim — that even mountain-sized sin is destroyed by dhyāna-yoga — sets the tone: this is not philosophical speculation but technical instruction.","Three principles to carry forward:","OM is the master mantra. The text's analysis of the praṇava (vv. 9-22) — its correspondences, its three syllables, the half-mātrā — is foundational to ALL later mantra-yoga. Master OM and you master the field.","The bow-arrow-target image (vv. 14-15) is the complete meditation manual. Praṇava bow, Self arrow, Brahman target — and the meditator must be \"apramatta\" (unwavering). All technique reduces to this.","The six cakras and kuṇḍalinī are functional, not anatomical. The Dhyāna Bindu's descriptions (vv. 42-46) describe energetic centers and currents — the practitioner experiences them in meditation, not in dissection. Take the practice as practice.","\"praṇavo dhanuḥ śaro hyātmā brahma tal-lakṣyam ucyate\" — \"The praṇava is the bow, the Self is the arrow, Brahman is said to be the target.\" (Dhyāna Bindu v. 14)","🪔"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
